IsoWizardBoot
=============

A native Qt 6 / QML application for writing boot images to USB devices on Linux.

Features
--------
- English interface with an ISO/IMG picker and drag-and-drop support.
- Settings > Languages: English and Norwegian Bokmål, applied immediately.
- Settings > Appearance: dark and light themes, including dialogs and menus.
- Language and theme preferences are saved between launches.
- Automatic USB discovery with model, device path and capacity.
- Explicit target selection and confirmation before overwriting a device.
- Actual progress, transfer speed, estimated time and an activity log.
- Optional SHA-256 verification by reading the contents back from USB.
- Cancellation with a controlled shutdown of the write operation.

Compatibility
-------------
The application writes uncompressed images directly to a whole USB device,
similar to dd. Use USB-compatible hybrid ISOs (such as Linux installation
images) or raw IMG files. The image determines the partition table, filesystem,
and BIOS/UEFI support. The app does not convert arbitrary ISOs into bootable
USB images, create persistence, or support standard Windows installation ISOs.
An .iso extension alone does not guarantee that an image can boot from USB.

Requirements
------------
Linux kernel 5.15 or later (disk sequence identification), CMake >= 3.16,
a C++17 compiler, Qt >= 6.5 with Quick, QuickControls2, Quick Dialogs and Qt Test
(for tests), util-linux (lsblk), and polkit (pkexec). A polkit authentication
agent must be running in the desktop session. The app runs without root;
only the writer requests administrator approval through pkexec.

Build and run
-------------
    cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    cmake --build build -j
    ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
    ./build/src/IsoWizardBoot

To omit tests, configure with -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF.
To preselect an image: ./build/src/IsoWizardBoot --image /path/to/image.iso

Install in KDE, GNOME and other Linux desktops
---------------------------------------------
From the project directory, build and install for your current user:
    make install

This installs to ~/.local by default: bin/IsoWizardBoot, the application-menu
entry under share/applications, and icons under share/icons/hicolor. Search
for IsoWizardBoot in your application launcher; it can also be pinned to a
panel or dock. No sudo is needed for this user-local installation. The launcher
uses the installed executable's absolute path, so ~/.local/bin need not be in
PATH. The app still needs the Qt runtime and other dependencies listed above.

For a system-wide installation:
    make PREFIX=/usr/local
    sudo cmake --install build --prefix /usr/local

CMake installation also works directly after building:
    cmake --install build --prefix "$HOME/.local"

Packaging can use DESTDIR for staging. Override BUILD_DIR, BUILD_TYPE or JOBS
when invoking make if needed. Desktop and icon caches refresh automatically
when their tools are available, except during DESTDIR package staging.

The icon is embedded in the binary for window/task-switcher use, and is also
installed for the application menu. A bare Linux executable may still display
a generic file icon in the file manager; launch through the installed menu entry.
The source artwork and generated size variants live in assets/icons.

Usage
-----
1. Select an ISO or IMG file.
2. Select the correct USB device. External USB hard drives are also listed.
3. If mounted, unmount all its partitions in your file manager while keeping
   the USB device connected. Store the source image on a different disk.
4. Choose whether to verify the contents, then click Write to USB.
5. Confirm the target and data erasure, then approve the system's administrator
   access dialog.
6. Wait for writing/verification to finish before ejecting the USB device.

Languages and themes
--------------------
Open Settings in the sidebar to change Languages or Appearance. English and
the dark theme are the defaults. Preferences are stored using Qt QSettings.

src/i18n/no-NB.i18n is a UTF-8 JSON translation file. Its translations object
maps English source strings to Norwegian Bokmål. The requested filename and
language ID are no-NB; the corresponding standard locale is nb-NO. Placeholder
strings such as %1 and %2 must be preserved. Missing keys fall back to English.
The file is embedded in the executable at build time; rebuild after editing it.
QML uses qsTr(), and a JSON-backed QTranslator applies the translations live.
Colors are defined in one theme palette at the top of src/qml/Main.qml.

Safety checks
-------------
Internal disks, read-only devices, and disks containing system mounts, swap,
or active device-mapper users are filtered out. Mounted targets are rejected.
The writer rechecks capacity, serial number and the kernel disk sequence after
approval, and verifies the disk sequence again after exclusive opening. It
checks source identity and opens the image with the invoking user's access.
The target must be an actual block device, not a regular file or symlink.

Tests
-----
The tests write only to temporary files. They cover device filtering,
byte-for-byte copying, readback corruption, cancellation, truncated sources,
file identity, UI states, the confirmation dialog, live language/theme changes,
and preference persistence. The UI test reads the device list but never starts
a real writer. No USB writing is performed by the test suite.

Capture the application without opening a desktop window:
    QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen QT_QUICK_BACKEND=software \
      ./build/src/IsoWizardBoot --screenshot /tmp/isowizardboot.png
